NWYD Windows neural runtime C ABI, version 2

All exported calls are serialized. Only one context may be active. Result zero
means success; all nonzero results include a bounded UTF-8 error message when
the caller supplies an error buffer. Structures must have exact struct_size
and abi_version values. Strings passed as uint16_t pointers are terminated
UTF-16. The adapter accepts only 64-bit Windows callers.

Probe checks the model and its exported Vulkan requirements, not GPU support.
A successful create is required to advertise device support. Probe does not
load a Vulkan layer, create a GPU device, or invoke a model evaluation.

Create borrows the caller's Vulkan instance, physical device, device and
recording command buffer. Device extensions returned by probe must already
be enabled. Command recording must occur outside a render pass. The caller
submits the creation commands and ensures completion before evaluating.
Each pass owns an independent feature and temporal history. Creation attempts
are spaced at least 250 ms apart, including the second pass. This is setup-only
work. The entire chain must initialize successfully; a partial chain is released.

CreateInfo appends a 32-byte tuning block at offset 72: five floats intensity,
tone, structure, skin, sharpness, followed by three uint32 values auto_mask,
style, passes. The total structure size is 104. NWYD defaults are 2.0, 2.0, 2.05,
-1.0, 0.25, 1, 2, 2. Style values 0/1/2 select Default/Natural/Cinematic. Intensity,
tone and structure accept 0..4, skin accepts -1..4, and sharpness accepts 0..1.
All floats must be finite. Auto-mask is 0/1; passes is 1/2. The other creation
controls use preset 0. Tuning is immutable for the context lifetime and is
applied before creating every feature. Sharpness is also applied after resource
binding on every evaluation.

Frame has reset at offset 144 and pass_index at offset 148; its size remains 152.
Evaluate borrows full-resolution depth R32 SFLOAT and motion RG16 SFLOAT.
A one-pass context reads RGBA8 UNORM and writes RGBA8 UNORM. A two-pass context
reads RGBA8 UNORM in pass 0 and writes RGBA16 UNORM; pass 1 reads that exact image
and view and writes a different RGBA16 UNORM image. No intermediate RGBA8
conversion is allowed. The caller owns both intermediate images and the final
composition. Every subresource is mip 0, layer 0, one mip and one layer, with
COLOR aspect. Inputs must be in SHADER_READ_ONLY_OPTIMAL and output in GENERAL.
Output must not alias input. Passes run strictly in ascending order, restarting
at 0 for the next frame. Both passes use identical depth/motion images and views
and the same reset value. Invalid arguments can be corrected and retried without
advancing the chain. Reset must be 0/1 and clears each feature's own history.
Motion is current-to-previous pixel displacement, with positive Y down;
depth is near 0/far 1. Raster and views remain alive until GPU completion.

Evaluate records commands only. The caller manages synchronization, queue
submission, and output transitions, and must discard an unsuccessful command
recording. A runtime evaluation failure disables the context until destroy.
The adapter does not wait or submit per frame. UI composition belongs after
evaluation. Native alpha/reactive preservation belongs to the caller.

Destroy requires the caller to have drained all submitted commands using the
context. The adapter never drains the device itself. Destroy restores caller
identity imports. The adapter and its inactive exception handler remain pinned
for the process lifetime because the upstream longjmp exception guard can leave
dispatcher references behind after an exception. GPU and model resources are
released when the context is destroyed; the code module remains mapped.
Successful destruction invalidates the context. Invalid handles must not be
reused, including when another context happens to occupy the same address.

The runtime directory contains user-supplied nvngx_dlssnr.dll. Optional NGX
core DLLs nvngx.dll/_nvngx.dll are rejected. Native Windows uses system NVAPI
and rejects local nvapi64.dll/dxgi.dll; Wine may use its local compatibility
DLLs for testing with an external runtime directory. The optional
bundled-neural-runtime build embeds explicitly supplied native Windows runtime
files and attribution, without Wine compatibility DLLs. Before probing, it
verifies and extracts the payload into the user's local NWYD cache. Default
builds continue loading external files. No build downloads NVIDIA binaries.
